# A glacial vale, 256 x 64 x 256 m — the landscape §5 of the voxel-shape
# research describes, built out of everything the four commits before it added.
#
#     loom render assets/test/moraine.loom --out moraine.png --size 1280x800
#     loom terrain assets/test/moraine.loom --from 50,96 --to 66,126
#
# **This is the composition scene, not a unit test.** `vale.loom` proves the
# recipe reaches the SDF in 192 chunks; this one asks what the whole vocabulary
# is worth in one frame, and it is `homestead`'s and `lanternhead`'s class of
# scene — it runs many systems at once, so it moves whenever any of them moves.
#
# In order, and this order is the recipe for a landscape rather than a habit:
#
#   1. the landform, from a `loom_terrain` recipe — the only op that can carry
#      erosion, a carved trough, a flattened pad and a guaranteed route;
#   2. the 3D half, which a height cannot express at all: an arch bored through
#      a displaced crag, and an undercut in its flank;
#   3. the built thing, off the axis, on the pad the recipe flattened;
#   4. the erratics — **imported meshes, not voxels**, for the reason below;
#   5. grass, which reads the terrain through `GroundGrid` and needs nothing
#      authored to know where it can grow.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rules this scene is standing on, each of which cost a render to learn
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **The erratics are meshes and that is the measured default.** A rock reads as
# rock at about 0.05 m, and 0.05 m over 256 m is 160 chunks per axis against a
# schema cap of 64 — detail rock at landscape scale is structurally not a
# single-volume problem. The research measured an imported scan at 30,223
# triangles and 55.8% concave surface, with its own atlas — these are the same
# LOD1 models `props.loom` catalogues; the voxel alternative at this pitch is
# `ceil(log2(A / voxel_size)) + 2 = 2` octaves of displacement, which is a
# potato with a crease. **Voxels win exactly three things** — craterable, CSG
# joinable to the ground, and sharing the terrain's collider and `exposure` —
# and none of them is wanted for a rock lying in a field.
#
# **The crag is the case where displacement does work**, and the difference is
# the ratio of amplitude to voxel size rather than anything about rock. At
# `amplitude = 1.6` and 0.5 m voxels the cap is `floor(log2(2A/v)) + 1 = 3`
# octaves, and three octaves over a 16 m mass is a fractured bluff. The same
# op at 1.6 m amplitude on a 1.6 m rock would be gravel.
#
# **Each mesh carries its OWN atlas.** These are baked per-model atlases, not
# tiling materials: two rocks from one set have different island layouts, so the
# wrong atlas smears rather than merely looking like the wrong stone. That bug
# is already in this project's history — `props.loom` records it.
#
# **`Light::intensity` is `d^2 / albedo`.** The lantern is 6.0, which is a
# surface 1.7 m away at albedo 0.5. The 100-800 the older scenes use is about
# 40x over, and against a fixed-point target it simply clamped, so nobody saw.
#
# **`Material.layer` is a slope rule**, so flat ground *always* gets the base
# material — the base is the meadow soil and the layer is the bedrock, which is
# the way round every scene but `lanternhead` (a quay deck is flat, and stone
# had to be its base). Its `slope` and `Grass::slope_cutoff` are both 0.50 and
# must stay equal: two thresholds on one hill draw two concentric rings.
#
# **0.50 and not the 0.62 this started at, because the rule's *band* is what
# bites, not its threshold.** `groundLayerWeight` is
# `1 - saturate((n.y - cutoff - wander) / 0.15)`, so rock is fully in at the
# cutoff and starts appearing 0.15 above it: at 0.62 that band is 24 to 52
# degrees, and this landscape's mean slope is 24. Two thirds of the vale came
# out bedrock. At 0.50 the band is 42 to 63 degrees, which is cliffs and the
# crag and nothing else.
#
# **Any scene with a `Grass` field owes its ground a green colour**, because
# blades fade to nothing with distance and the far field is then the ground
# alone. `meadow`'s brown soil read as ploughed earth from an orbit.
#
# **Texels/m is resolution x uv_scale.** The soil is 4096 at 0.35 = 1434/m and
# the rock 1024 at 2.2 = 2253/m, both far past the ~400/m a near field can
# show. The rock started at 0.5 (512/m, a 2 m tile) and read as crazy paving
# on the crag — a cobble atlas has a physical size, and tiling it to the
# texel budget rather than to the size of a cobble is what makes it wallpaper.
#
# **A `Subtract` sphere makes a dish only if its centre is ABOVE the ground.**
# Everything inside the sphere is removed, so a sphere centred a metre *under*
# the surface excavates to its own bottom — the first seat for the big erratic
# was a 7 m crater. For a hollow `D` deep, `centre_y = ground - D + r`, and it
# is `2 * sqrt(r^2 - (r - D)^2)` wide: r = 4, D = 0.75, so 4.8 m across.
#
# **What is NOT here.** No water: `World::water()` is one body and a
# `WaterBody` has no extent, so a tarn in the trough is an infinite plane that
# floods the whole northern half as well. No rain, no scatter, no trees — each
# would be a second new thing in a frame whose first new thing is the landform.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cost — measured, and it is not where the research said it would be
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `loom render ... --size 640x400`, twice each, on this machine:
#
#     the whole scene                       11.43 / 11.33 s
#     with the grass field deleted           7.04 / 7.04     -> grass  4.3 s
#     with the one displaced sphere deleted  7.52 / 7.31     -> that op 4.0 s
#     the recipe alone (`loom terrain`)      0.16 s
#
# So the 1024-chunk landform bake, the mesh, the props and the frame together
# are about 3.1 s, and **one displaced primitive costs more than all of it**.
# `Displace::gradient_bound` is 4.0 per octave and `Volume::bake` takes the
# max over ops, so three octaves widen `reach` for every chunk in the volume,
# not just the chunks the crag is in. That is the strongest argument in the
# scene for the research's "mesh is the default" rule, and it is a cost
# argument rather than a fidelity one.
#
# **The grass field's 4.3 s is not the blades.** Measured: density 90 -> 40
# changes nothing (11.43 -> 11.26 s) and half_extent 24 -> 20 changes nothing.
# It is `GroundGrid`'s march of the SDF, which is per field and scales with
# the *volume* it is marching, not with the field over it. Nothing in the
# scene tunes it; a smaller volume would.
#
# All of it is load-time. `LOOM_GPU_TIMING=1` puts grass at 0.054 ms a frame.
# This is nonetheless the most expensive scene in `SCENES` — `terrain_stress`
# is 2.1 s with twice the chunks — and the reason it is worth that is in the
# `SCENES` entry.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "accf696e-2ea0-4b6b-9dec-9210944e0485"

# --- ground maps ----------------------------------------------------------

[[asset]]
key = "grass_ground_albedo"
id = "7084a98d-9451-4267-918d-ce480b9f1157"
